<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Italian War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Italian War of Independence was fought in 1859 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>. Austrian defeats at Magenta and Solferino led to the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> place it among the defeats that pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy towards the reforms and compromise that created Austria-Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Second Italian War of Independence" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adolphe Yvon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yvon_Bataille_de_Solferino_Compiegne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>'s defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a>, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour</a>, looked for allies, partly by taking Piedmont into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>. Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> were sympathetic but would not act against Austria. On 14 January 1858 Felice Orsini led an attempt on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, which brought sympathy for Italian unity and left the emperor determined to help Piedmont.</p><p>After a covert meeting at Plombières on 21 July 1858, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> signed a secret alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 28 January 1859. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> would help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> if it were attacked, in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and Savoy. Cavour provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> with military manoeuvres near the border; Sardinia mobilised on 9 March 1859, Austria on 9 April, and an Austrian ultimatum of 23 April demanding demobilisation led to war on 26 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The French army, about 170,000 strong under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in the first massive military use of railways. The Austrian commander, Count Gyulai, was cautious, and heavy rain, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields flooded by the Piedmontese, slowed his advance. Napoleon III arrived at Alessandria on 14 May and took command. After the Battle of Montebello on 20 May and the Battle of Turbigo, the allies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and defeated the Austrians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magenta">Battle of Magenta</a>. Gyulai was relieved, and Emperor Franz Josef took command himself.</p><p>The two armies met by accident around Solferino on 24 June, and after a day of confused fighting the French broke through and the Austrians withdrew into the fortresses of the Quadrilateral. Giuseppe Garibaldi's Hunters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> meanwhile defeated the Austrians at Varese and Como. A ceasefire was agreed on 8 July, and the two emperors met at Villafranca di Verona on 11 July; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> was signed the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> made peace partly because the Quadrilateral would be costly to attack, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was exposed in his absence, his actions were criticised at home and he feared involvement of the German states. Under the armistice <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, apart from Mantua and Legnago, passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to France and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Cavour resigned in protest at a deal made behind Sardinia's back. The terms never took effect, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> occupied the central Italian states and would not restore their rulers.</p><p>On 22 March 1860 the United Provinces of Central Italy were annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> received Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. Garibaldi, a native of Nice, opposed the cession, which led to his expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Austria's influence in Italy was severely weakened. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> mobilised 132,000 men but never fought, and the weaknesses it found led to military reforms that underlay its victories over Austria in 1866 and France in 1870-71.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Italian War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…but the defeats of the 1850s and 1860s forced a reconstruction of the imperial structure: the loss of Lombardy in the <b>Second Italian War of Independence</b> (1859), and the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, which excluded Austria from German affairs after the defeat at Königgrätz and cost it Venetia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…in 1854, leaving the regent Louise Marie of Artois to govern for the young Robert I. During the <b>Second Italian War of Independence</b> the regent left in June 1859, provisional authorities, later led by Luigi Carlo Farini, joined the duchy to the United Provinces of Central Italy, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
